1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,180
Hi, I'm David Lim.

2
00:00:01,560 --> 00:00:02,880
This is my week two assessment.

3
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:05,060
I call it SG Commute Pulse.

4
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:07,460
This is a real problem I have.

5
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,520
I have a calendar event
somewhere in Singapore.

6
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:12,080
To work out when to
leave, I check three apps.

7
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:13,620
One for the route, one
for the bus, one for the

8
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,380
weather. None of them know my calendar.

9
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,160
So I built one pipeline
just to answer one question.

10
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:19,320
When do I need to leave?

11
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:21,900
It reads my next event
and finds the location.

12
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:27,000
Then it pulls routes, live bus times
train alert, and weather.

13
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:28,740
We store all of that in DuckDB.

14
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:32,120
SQL picks the best route
and work out my leave by

15
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:35,740
time. Then a model adds
a predicted delay on top.

16
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:37,600
Next, I will show you how
the system is being put

17
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:38,200
together.

18
00:00:39,570 --> 00:00:40,770
It has three layers.

19
00:00:40,890 --> 00:00:43,550
I kept them separated because
they do different job.

20
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:45,130
Layer one is the data pipeline.

21
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:49,750
It pulls data, store it, and
run a SQL transformation.

22
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:53,970
That is the core data engineering work
Layer two is the machine learning.

23
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:55,830
One model predicted delay.

24
00:00:55,910 --> 00:00:59,670
A second model predicted
how crowded the bus will be.

25
00:00:59,830 --> 00:01:00,750
Layer three is serving.

26
00:01:01,210 --> 00:01:05,470
A dashboard that refreshes
every 60 seconds and an API for

27
00:01:05,510 --> 00:01:10,050
anything else. I also store the data twice

28
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:13,530
on purpose. The raw pulls
goes into Parquet files.

29
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:16,770
It keeps whatever each API gave me.

30
00:01:17,030 --> 00:01:18,810
The clean version goes into DuckDB.

31
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:22,890
That is what the dashboard
and the API reads.

32
00:01:23,190 --> 00:01:26,630
Next, where the data comes from.

33
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:31,110
Over here, you will
see my config template.

34
00:01:31,350 --> 00:01:32,910
It holds the placeholder for my keys.

35
00:01:33,110 --> 00:01:35,810
The real file is not
in the repo and is not

36
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:36,830
on this screen.

37
00:01:37,170 --> 00:01:38,170
I have five data sources.

38
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:39,870
I will go through one at a time.

39
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:41,810
The first one is the Google calendar.

40
00:01:42,270 --> 00:01:43,330
This is my starting point.

41
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:45,510
It gives me the next
event and its location.

42
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:47,560
The second one will be OneMap.

43
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:50,100
It turns the location into coordinates.

44
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:51,940
Then gives me up to three routes.

45
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:55,480
Each route has a duration, a
walking distance and a fare.

46
00:01:55,780 --> 00:02:00,460
Next will be LTA DataMall This gives
me the live bus arrival times and

47
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,580
the MRT disruption alerts

48
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:05,260
The fourth is you won't see it here.

49
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:08,360
It is from data .graph .sg.

50
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:11,480
A two -hour weather forecast for 47 area.

51
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:13,480
It is open, so no key needed.

52
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:15,180
That's why you won't see it here.

53
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:16,940
The fifth one is my starting location.

54
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:20,660
I have an interesting story for
this, when I was experimenting

55
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:24,100
to see if I can get my
location without asking for

56
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:28,780
GPS permission. I use a
free service that guesses

57
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:31,400
it from my internet connection.

58
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:35,100
On the day that I'm testing,
it put me at Woodlands,

59
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:39,600
then Jurong I was physically
sitting in Punggol the whole time.

60
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:44,520
Later, I then realized it was
reading my internet provider, not

61
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:48,800
my device. Now I use a
postal code that I have to

62
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:52,440
set myself. The free
service is only a backup.

63
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:54,980
Next is the pipeline itself.

64
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,740
Let's move to my pipeline.

65
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:01,500
Okay, this is Airflow,

66
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:05,820
and this is my pipeline as a graph.

67
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:09,520
Seven tasks running in order,
check the schema, pull the data,

68
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:13,500
transform it, predict and fill
in what actually happened, then

69
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:15,740
evaluate the model.

70
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:18,180
This is the interesting part
that I would like to highlight

71
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:19,160
in this Airflow.

72
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:22,280
The pipeline runs every 30
minutes, but I do not want

73
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:24,080
it to test my model every 30 minutes.

74
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:26,360
So I added a gate, which is over here.

75
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:30,180
Only at 8am is allowed
through to evaluation.

76
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:32,860
You can see it working
all the way, but the last

77
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:33,900
one is gate.

78
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:39,530
In Airflow, this gate is
called short circuit operator.

79
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:41,510
The other thing that I
want to show you is what

80
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:42,930
happened if API fails.

81
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:44,950
So let's go back to VS code.

82
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:49,320
If we focus on this part
of the script, LTA API

83
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:51,460
sometimes fail during rush
hour, so I don't want to give

84
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:53,000
up on the first failure.

85
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:54,700
I try three times.

86
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:57,760
I wait one second, then
two, then four seconds.

87
00:03:58,220 --> 00:03:59,660
The wait double each time.

88
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:02,060
The pattern is called
exponential back off.

89
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:06,480
Without it, one failed call at
8am will kill the whole run.

90
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:08,940
Okay, the next part
that I want to show you,

91
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:12,220
I made my pipeline safe to run again.

92
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:14,520
Here is why it matters.

93
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:17,740
If the pipeline crashes
halfway, I just want it to run

94
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:20,620
it again. I do not want a
second copy of everything.

95
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:23,560
Let me prove it by running
this in the terminal.

96
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:28,620
We have 12, 27, 91, and 124.

97
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:32,380
This is the calendar event,
route option, route leg, and bus

98
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:34,920
arrival. Now let me ingest it again.

99
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:36,520
The numbers should not double.

100
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,120
In fact, some of the numbers
should not move at all.

101
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:42,520
Lastly, let me run this
again in the terminal.

102
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:47,920
Okay, as you can see, still
12, still 27, still 91,

103
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:50,300
but the bus arrival changed to 128.

104
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:53,020
12, 27, 91, 124, 128.

105
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:54,240
They did not double.

106
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:56,040
There's something new.

107
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:58,340
That is because I do not add a new row.

108
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:00,820
I replace the row that is already there.

109
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:02,340
It matches on the primary key.

110
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:05,380
That word for this is idempotency.

111
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:08,700
There is one table that
grows, and I want two, which

112
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:09,980
is the bus arrival.

113
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:13,820
Every fetch is a snapshot
of that moment so that fetch

114
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:15,740
time is part of its key.

115
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:19,980
A new run adds a new snapshot
instead of replacing the old one

116
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:23,160
That gives me a time
series I can look back to.

117
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:28,240
Same SQL command, different behavior
on purpose because I choose different

118
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:30,800
keys. One honest note about storage.

119
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:33,300
My Parquet layer is a staging layer.

120
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:35,840
One file per source per day.

121
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:39,180
It's not a full archive
of every raw API response.

122
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:42,680
That is something that I
will fix in the future.

123
00:05:42,980 --> 00:05:45,540
Next is the SQL.

124
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:48,380
This is where the
recommendation is actually made.

125
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:49,840
This is the SQL view.

126
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:53,480
It joins my route option to
the weather at the destination

127
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:56,460
and to any active train alert.

128
00:05:57,140 --> 00:05:58,620
The leave-by time is simple.

129
00:06:00,190 --> 00:06:04,710
To rank the routes, I use a window

130
00:06:04,750 --> 00:06:09,190
function. It numbers the route
for each event, best one first.

131
00:06:09,790 --> 00:06:11,170
But I do not just pick the fastest.

132
00:06:11,610 --> 00:06:14,390
If it's raining and a
route has a long walk, I

133
00:06:14,430 --> 00:06:15,930
push that route down.

134
00:06:16,230 --> 00:06:19,250
So a slightly slower, drier route can win.

135
00:06:19,450 --> 00:06:22,670
the “Why chosen” line is also SQL.

136
00:06:23,050 --> 00:06:25,970
It compares the route against
the other route and pick one

137
00:06:26,010 --> 00:06:27,030
of nine reasons.

138
00:06:27,230 --> 00:06:30,790
disruption warning are filtered
to the train lines my routes

139
00:06:30,830 --> 00:06:32,670
actually uses.

140
00:06:33,350 --> 00:06:37,470
So a bus only route shows
no train warning at all.

141
00:06:37,670 --> 00:06:42,150
So if you want to see how
it's been calculated, the

142
00:06:42,190 --> 00:06:44,250
leave by formula, it will be here.

143
00:06:45,420 --> 00:06:46,820
Okay, now the dashboard.

144
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:48,680
This is live output.

145
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:50,880
It refreshes every 60 seconds.

146
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:54,100
It makes no API call.

147
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:55,940
Here is the leave by time.

148
00:06:57,010 --> 00:06:58,590
Here is the journey duration.

149
00:06:59,310 --> 00:06:59,970
Here is the fare.

150
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:03,020
The weather and the disruption
warning comes from SQL.

151
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:05,760
And each route option
has its own prediction.

152
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:09,100
So the alternatives are also scored too

153
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:15,180
There is also a model health panel here.

154
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:18,140
I will come back to this in
the machine learning section.

155
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:20,340
Now above, I hit here.

156
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:21,900
It took me two tries to fix.

157
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:24,340
DuckDB only allows one writer at a time.

158
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:28,140
My first version opened one connection
when the dashboard started and

159
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:29,680
kept reusing it.

160
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:32,080
So the dashboard never
saw new data at all.

161
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:35,480
I removed that, but it still
broke in a different way.

162
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:38,860
The dashboard was holding the
connection open while it slept for

163
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:43,500
60 seconds. So my pipeline
could not write for almost the

164
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:44,520
whole minute.

165
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:48,000
The fix was to change the order.

166
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:52,520
Run all query first, close the
connection, then draw the page and sleep.

167
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:55,860
Now it's open for a millisecond
instead of a minute.

168
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:57,520
I tested both ways.

169
00:07:57,560 --> 00:07:59,800
The old version gives a log error.

170
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:00,840
The new one doesn't.

171
00:08:01,140 --> 00:08:02,300
Now, machine learning.

172
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:03,340
I have two models.

173
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:07,900
I want to spend most of
the section on one mistake

174
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:10,060
I found and one feature I made myself.

175
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:13,180
The first mistake, my first
model tried to predict the whole

176
00:08:13,220 --> 00:08:16,860
journey time. And I also give
OneMap journey time as one

177
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:17,700
of the input.

178
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:21,740
Then I checked which input
the model was actually using.

179
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,820
That one input was 87 .5 % of the model.

180
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:27,660
So my model was mostly copying OneMap.

181
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:30,200
It was not learning anything about delay.

182
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:32,600
There was a second problem too.

183
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:36,450
A random forest can only predict
value it has already seen

184
00:08:36,490 --> 00:08:37,290
in the training.

185
00:08:37,830 --> 00:08:41,670
So every long route got
kept at a same number.

186
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:43,850
So I changed that question.

187
00:08:44,070 --> 00:08:45,170
I was asking it.

188
00:08:45,210 --> 00:08:48,650
Instead, predicting the whole journey,
the model now predicts only the

189
00:08:48,690 --> 00:08:52,910
extra delay. Then my final
answer is OneMap time plus

190
00:08:52,950 --> 00:08:54,750
that predicted delay.

191
00:08:55,190 --> 00:08:58,130
After the change, OneMap feature importance
was about 5%.

192
00:08:58,170 --> 00:09:00,490
Now other things matter more.

193
00:09:00,750 --> 00:09:04,230
Rush hour, bus timing, rain,
and the ceiling is gone because

194
00:09:04,270 --> 00:09:07,370
the data is anchored to OneMap's number.

195
00:09:07,730 --> 00:09:10,290
If you want the technical
name for the problem, it is

196
00:09:10,330 --> 00:09:12,650
sometimes called target proxy dominance.

197
00:09:13,390 --> 00:09:16,470
What I was trying to predict
was mostly built out of

198
00:09:16,510 --> 00:09:18,250
one of my own input.

199
00:09:20,420 --> 00:09:23,660
Now the feature I am most pleased with.

200
00:09:23,780 --> 00:09:25,260
LTA tell me two things.

201
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:27,500
The next bus and the bus after that.

202
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:30,520
Say the next bus is three minutes away.

203
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:33,720
And if the one after it
was six minutes away, the

204
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:35,260
service is running normally.

205
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:39,720
But if the one after is
20 minutes away, there is

206
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:40,880
a big gap behind it.

207
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,860
People keep arriving at the
stop during those 20 minutes.

208
00:09:45,080 --> 00:09:49,580
So the bus that turn up is
fuller and slower toward.

209
00:09:49,780 --> 00:09:51,480
So I made a new column.

210
00:09:51,720 --> 00:09:55,100
The gap between the bus one
and bus two, three minutes,

211
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:56,140
then 20 minutes.

212
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:57,360
The gap is 17.

213
00:09:57,780 --> 00:10:01,910
I called it bus headway gap
Here's the point.

214
00:10:02,430 --> 00:10:05,370
In both cases, the first
bus is three minutes away.

215
00:10:05,750 --> 00:10:06,990
The number looks identical.

216
00:10:07,190 --> 00:10:10,210
A model that only see the
first bus cannot tell those

217
00:10:10,250 --> 00:10:12,250
situation apart. Mine can.

218
00:10:12,670 --> 00:10:14,590
LTA already sent me both numbers.

219
00:10:14,730 --> 00:10:16,770
I was only using the
second one for display.

220
00:10:17,210 --> 00:10:20,090
So it cost me no extra API calls.

221
00:10:20,510 --> 00:10:24,990
It feeds both models and all
three routes option separately.

222
00:10:25,460 --> 00:10:26,700
Then the full loop.

223
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:27,880
Train the model.

224
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:28,620
Predict.

225
00:10:29,180 --> 00:10:30,920
Fill in what actually happened.

226
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:32,900
Then evaluate. We don't need that.

227
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:36,920
Let's see. Filling in what
actually happened is the hard part.

228
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:39,480
For seven days, I lock on
my own, leave and arrive

229
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:40,640
time on my phone.

230
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:42,060
Those are real observation.

231
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:44,420
But I want to be clear
about what they are not.

232
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:46,060
They are not GPS track.

233
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:48,640
I have no trip tracking in this system.

234
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:51,440
In fact, while checking them
today, I found I had over

235
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:54,940
record them by nine minutes
for some of the session and

236
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:55,980
I corrected that.

237
00:10:56,460 --> 00:10:59,500
Other rows are reconstructed instead.

238
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:04,060
The schedule plus the
bus, which I actually saw,

239
00:11:04,580 --> 00:11:06,460
plus a published gap for trains.

240
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:07,760
I saw which

241
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:10,880
method was used for every single row.

242
00:11:11,260 --> 00:11:14,600
So a hand-logged time and
estimate never get mixed up.

243
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:17,700
The evaluator runs once a day.

244
00:11:17,820 --> 00:11:20,740
It measure my average error
over the past seven days.

245
00:11:20,960 --> 00:11:22,660
Right now, it is 6.1 minutes.

246
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,640
My warning threshold is 10.

247
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:26,960
So the dashboard says healthy.

248
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:29,840
But I also built a
comparison that matters more.

249
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:33,780
What if I use no model
or just trusted OneMap?

250
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:35,760
That gives me 3.7 minutes.

251
00:11:36,140 --> 00:11:38,840
So today my model
does not beat just trusting OneMap.

252
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:42,660
I would rather say that than hide it.

253
00:11:45,030 --> 00:11:48,490
Although I checked why and the
comparison is not fair yet,

254
00:11:48,850 --> 00:11:51,350
my model was scored on nine outcomes.

255
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:53,650
OneMap was only scored on three.

